  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1131 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nMonday through Friday:\n\nIn the wake of the cold front Monday temperatures will be a bit\ncooler with high temperatures only climbing into the mid 50s to\nmid 60s. Heading through the rest of the week into the weekend\nthere will be an upper level trough that starts in the Pacific\nNorthwest advances east into the Rockies before pulling\nnortheast toward the Hudson Bay. As the upper level trough moves\nthrough the Rockies a sfc low is expected to develop on the lee\nside and follow a similar path. As this trough and eventual sfc\nlow moves east northeast, southerly winds will return to\nsouthern Wisconsin Tuesday and Wednesday. This will drive\ntemperatures back up in the upper 60s to low 70s. Despite \nwarming temperatures the better moisture return and lift looks \nto remain just to the northwest across central and northern \nWisconsin, which keeps POPs around 5% of less. Unless we see the\ntrack of the sfc low shift further east or south with time its \nlikely that southern Wisconsin will remain dry until mid week \n(roughly Wednesday into Wednesday night).\n\nAs the sfc low moves toward the Hudson Bay Wednesday and\nThursday another cold front is expected to move southeast down\nthe state. The cold front is should be a bit on the weaker side,\nbut guidance is showing better available moisture. The sfc\nlow should have occluded by this point so lift and frontogenesis\nwill be our limiting factor, but overall there is growing \nconfidence on light rain through Thursday morning. The timing of\nthe rain should tighten up with time if this trend continues. \nBeyond this point and looking ahead into the weekend, this low \npressure system and upper level trough does look to signal a \npattern change for the Great Lakes Region. Guidance signals \nthat the ridging that has been parked over the central and \neastern CONUS will start to move and break down, this will give \nus more chances for rain heading into the weekend. \n\nPatterson",
